 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "This is a Flask-like framework called `tessel`. The order is:\n\n1. `Tessel.__call__` (`src/tessel/app.py:1618`) just delegates to `wsgi_entry`.\n2. `Tessel.wsgi_entry` (`app.py:1566`) is where the work starts:\n   - It calls `request_context(environ)` (`app.py:1501`), which calls `AppContext.from_environ` (`src/tessel/ctx.py:340`) to build the context and request object.\n   - It then calls `ctx.push()`.\n3. `AppContext.push` (`ctx.py:416`) does three things in order:\n   - It sets the context var and sends the `appcontext_pushed` signal.\n   - It opens the session through `_get_session` (`ctx.py:381`).\n   - It calls `match_request` (`ctx.py:405`). This runs URL routing and stores `url_rule` and `view_args`, or a `routing_exception` if routing failed.\n4. `wsgi_entry` calls `run_full_request(ctx)` (`app.py:992`). It sends the `request_started` signal, then calls `preprocess_request`.\n5. `preprocess_request` (`app.py:1366`) runs the URL-value preprocessors and the before-request functions. If one of them returns a value, that value is used and the view is skipped.\n6. `run_view` (`app.py:966`) raises any stored routing exception, handles automatic OPTIONS responses, then runs `self.ensure_sync(self.view_functions[rule.endpoint])(**view_args)` (`app.py:990`). That last call is your view function.\n\nExceptions raised in steps 4–6 go to `route_user_fault`. After the view returns, `finalize_request` (`app.py:1021`) builds the response.",
